# Issues #979 and #980, the execution half. Two readers did not honour `spec/grammar.md:34`'s
# *"within a single expression … newlines are insignificant"*: the `value`/`of` interception of the
# Heritage reader (#979), and the `.field` postfix (#980).
#
# The check twin proves these spellings are not DIAGNOSED. This one proves they MEAN what their
# one-line spellings mean, which no diagnostic can show — #980's defect dropped the `.field`
# entirely and left a bare variable read, a *smaller* tree rather than a malformed one, and #944's
# sibling defect in this same slice reparsed a dictionary silently. So every spelling below prints
# beside its one-line twin and the two payloads must be identical.
#
# `execute()` runs `parse()` and never `check()`, so the runtime holds its own copy of these rules.
# The reader operates on a dict, so Heritage also needs Data (`spec/conformance.md:305`).
:d = { a: 7 b: "a" }
:k = "a"
:nested = { inner: { x: 9 } }

# #979 — a newline between `value` and `of`.
print value
of :d for key :k
print value of :d for key :k

# The same, wrapped in parentheses.
print (value
of :d for key :k)
print (value of :d for key :k)

# The same, inside a dict entry value — the container where a misreading is silent.
print { a: value
of :d for key :k }
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# UPPERCASE, because keywords are case-insensitive (`spec/grammar.md:13`) and a continuation that
# compared surface text would break only this spelling.
print VALUE
OF :d FOR KEY :k
print VALUE OF :d FOR KEY :k

# A newline at every internal slot of the reader at once.
print value
of
:d
for
key
:k
print value of :d for key :k

# #980 — a `.field` postfix on the next line, inside a parenthesized group. Under the defect this
# raised two `ol-unmatched-paren` on parentheses that are correctly matched. In THIS container the
# field itself survived; in a list, dict entry or at the top level the defect dropped it instead,
# which is why the rows below cover all four rather than generalising from one.
print ( :d
.a )
print ( :d .a )

# The same inside a list literal and a dict entry value.
print [ :d
.a ]
print [ :d .a ]

print { x: :d
.a }
print { x: :d .a }

# A chain split across two newlines.
print ( :nested
.inner
.x )
print ( :nested .inner .x )

# Control: a newline still terminates a statement, so these stay TWO instructions and two prints.
print 1
print 2
